1. Import Statements:

from sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String
from setup_database import Base
from sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String:

Column:
This is used to define columns in a SQLAlchemy model.
It specifies the type and other attributes of the column in the table.

Integer:
This is a data type that SQLAlchemy uses to define integer columns.

String:
This is a data type for defining string columns in SQLAlchemy.

from setup_database import Base:

Base:
This is the base class from setup_database.py that your model class will inherit from.
It allows SQLAlchemy to map the class to a table in the database.

The Base class is created using the declarative_base() function in setup_database.py.
All classes that represent database tables must inherit from Base.


2. The User Class:

class User(Base):
    __tablename__ = 'users'
    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)
    name = Column(String)
    email = Column(String)

class User(Base):
This defines a class named User that inherits from Base.
By inheriting from Base, the User class is mapped to a table in the database,
and each instance of User corresponds to a row in the table.

The User class will represent a table in the database with columns corresponding
to the attributes defined inside the class.


3. __tablename__ Attribute:

__tablename__ = 'users'

__tablename__:
This special class attribute defines the name of the table that this class will represent in the database.
In this case, the class User will be mapped to a table named users in the database.

This tells SQLAlchemy that the User class corresponds to the users table, which will hold user-related data.


4. Columns Definition:

id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)
name = Column(String)
email = Column(String)
id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True):

This defines a column named id in the users table.
The column will store integer values (Integer data type).
primary_key=True specifies that this column will be the primary key for the table,
which means it will uniquely identify each row in the users table.

id is typically used as a unique identifier for each user in the table (e.g., a user’s id might be 1, 2, etc.).

name = Column(String):
This defines a column named name in the users table.
The column will store string values (String data type).
This column will store the name of the user (e.g., "David", "Joey").

email = Column(String):
This defines a column named email in the users table.
The column will also store string values, specifically the user’s email address.


5. Putting it Together:

User Class as a Table:
When you define the User class as shown above, SQLAlchemy uses it as a template
to create a table in the database (if it doesn't already exist).

The class User will be mapped to the users table with three columns: id, name, and email.

Creating the Table:
If you haven't created the table yet, you can use Base.metadata.create_all(engine)
to create the users table in the database.

SQLAlchemy will automatically generate the necessary SQL statements to create the table
based on the class definition.

Example of Interacting with the User Table:
Once you have defined the User class, you can interact with the users table like this:

# Create a new user instance
new_user = User(name="Joey", email="joey@gmail.com")

# Add the new user to the session
session.add(new_user)

# Commit the transaction to save the user to the database
session.commit()
This code will add a new row to the users table with the name "Joey" and the email "joey@gmail.com".

Summary:
User class:
This class represents a table in the database.

Base:
The User class inherits from Base, allowing SQLAlchemy to map it to a database table.

__tablename__:
Specifies the table name in the database (users).

Columns:
id, name, and email are columns in the users table,
each defined using Column with specific data types (Integer, String).